#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546976 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8566d65-0dce-59ca-b019-ddcb02c68e67" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Normal Approximation to Approximate a Binomial Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bc0687c-5235-5fca-8c76-9ff8fac5b144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.055" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546310} {:answer/id #uuid "2d30494f-d6ab-58f3-870e-1e63da9ec982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.035" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546348} {:answer/id #uuid "86466a98-40c1-52d1-b985-e139aa59131e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546547} {:answer/id #uuid "cd74bf35-5ee1-5c09-9af1-09b03f9a0630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.050" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546706} {:answer/id #uuid "d5deec87-c49a-59df-8e7a-1cfb510a6f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.045" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5deec87-c49a-59df-8e7a-1cfb510a6f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.045" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546725} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f87428c-0e9e-5478-a109-4d5ef2880e2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2264ca28-a89d-5082-9433-8c9a3ae7f639" :question/math-academy-id "q-132293" :question/problem "Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, approximate the probability of getting $30$ odd numbers on $70$ rolls of a $6$-sided die. Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0968\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0951\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0934\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1151\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1131\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1112\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1357\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1335\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1314\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1587\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1562\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1539\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.09\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0854\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0838\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0823\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1020\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1003\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0985\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1210\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1190\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1170\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1423\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1401\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1379\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=70$ and $p=0.5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =70\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =35 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =70\\cdot (1-0.5) \\\\  & =35 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(70)(0.5)=35\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(70)(0.5)(1-0.5)=17.5.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(35,17.5).$\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability $P(X=30).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=30)≈P(29.5\\le Y\\le 30.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(29.5\\le Y\\le 30.5) & =P(\\frac{29.5-35}{\\sqrt{17.5}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{30.5-35}{\\sqrt{17.5}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.31\\le Z\\le -1.08) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -1.08)-P(Z<-1.31) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -1.08)-P(Z\\le -1.31) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.08)-Φ(-1.31) \\\\  & =0.1401-0.0951 \\\\  & =0.045\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=30)≈0.045.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0469.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115317 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91946709-3ca8-5501-be63-336b06f6c305" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Normal Approximation to Approximate a Binomial Probability Over an Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cca4320-57c1-5b80-a07b-265d76f41a1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.575" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546312} {:answer/id #uuid "5b68ab59-554f-510e-b7b7-e4327a68e889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.625" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546450} {:answer/id #uuid "7a6c5598-a12c-551c-bb3e-56ef60b41e3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.559" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546524} {:answer/id #uuid "bd077aeb-6760-5099-a288-912ce3b76411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.595" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546675} {:answer/id #uuid "fd7b869e-897e-5d45-a2a6-59726fb76269" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.611" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd077aeb-6760-5099-a288-912ce3b76411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.595" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546675} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0fd26bd-399a-54be-b0ad-a1147ffe314c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0507dec-a4ee-5084-b5d0-2e0410c8955c" :question/math-academy-id "q-132494" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼B(100,0.5),$ use the normal approximation to the binomial distribution to approximate $P(43<X<53).$ Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0668\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0655\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0643\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0808\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0793\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0778\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0968\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0951\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0934\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6554\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6591\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6628\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6915\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6950\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6985\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7258\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7291\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7324\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=100$ and $p=0.5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =100\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =50 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =100\\cdot (1-0.5) \\\\  & =50 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(100)(0.5)=50\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(100)(0.5)(1-0.5)=25.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(50,25).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(43<X<53).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(43<X<53)=P(44\\le X\\le 52)≈P(43.5\\le Y\\le 52.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(43.5\\le Y\\le 52.5) & =P(\\frac{43.5-50}{\\sqrt{25}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{52.5-50}{\\sqrt{25}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.30\\le Z\\le 0.50) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.50)-P(Z<-1.30) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.50)-P(Z\\le -1.30) \\\\  & =Φ(0.50)-Φ(-1.30) \\\\  & =0.6915-0.0968 \\\\  & =0.5947\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(43<X<53)≈0.595.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.5947.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115990 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8566d65-0dce-59ca-b019-ddcb02c68e67" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Normal Approximation to Approximate a Binomial Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9bd3e3d-a5e0-52d8-a314-8bad4acc0d83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.015" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114878} {:answer/id #uuid "ec60f0b6-83bc-5c04-8c19-7750d1978cd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.021" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114879} {:answer/id #uuid "f534f026-e760-5700-b06d-0cbe06c3b5c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.090" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114880} {:answer/id #uuid "41a1093b-d27d-5747-947e-e40b93d0c024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.013" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114881} {:answer/id #uuid "757c4a02-d422-5930-9948-aeac51a81276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.010" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "757c4a02-d422-5930-9948-aeac51a81276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.010" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114882} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6054f5d-d816-5aba-ad9f-7006e0de6f71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "58190d0f-af25-525b-8820-0213217d6f88" :question/math-academy-id "q-95099" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼B(100,0.4),$ use the normal approximation to the binomial distribution to approximate $P(X=50).$ Give your final answer to three decimal places\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.9738\\,$ | $\\,0.9744\\,$ | $\\,0.9750\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9793\\,$ | $\\,0.9798\\,$ | $\\,0.9803\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9838\\,$ | $\\,0.9842\\,$ | $\\,0.9846\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.9875\\,$ | $\\,0.9878\\,$ | $\\,0.9881\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9904\\,$ | $\\,0.9906\\,$ | $\\,0.9909\\,$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=100$ and $p=0.4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =100\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =40 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =100\\cdot (1-0.4) \\\\  & =60 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(100)(0.4)=40\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(100)(0.4)(1-0.4)=24.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(40,24).$\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability $P(X=50).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=50)≈P(49.5\\le Y\\le 50.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(49.5\\le Y\\le 50.5) & =P(\\frac{49.5-40}{\\sqrt{24}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{50.5-40}{\\sqrt{24}}) \\\\  & ≈P(1.94\\le Z\\le 2.14) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.14)-P(Z<1.94) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.14)-P(Z\\le 1.94) \\\\  & =Φ(2.14)-Φ(1.94) \\\\  & =0.9838-0.9738 \\\\  & =0.010\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=50)≈0.010.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0102.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546963 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115315 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f5f60f5-007d-5b1c-b3bc-f352175dcc91" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations Where a Normal Approximation is Appropriate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f393495-fa9e-5fba-89ad-9cf9d34fc9dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546281} {:answer/id #uuid "25db45d4-7629-56bc-a46c-f9583b199fb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546337} {:answer/id #uuid "6e1b6156-1a88-565f-af03-ff5611e465e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546494} {:answer/id #uuid "9100b24b-0cf7-5ad7-b5ab-d45a5d1d1454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546570} {:answer/id #uuid "f983dc56-76cf-5144-8e25-eee8a8acf15c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f983dc56-76cf-5144-8e25-eee8a8acf15c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546809} :answer-field/id #uuid "26bcb8a5-120e-5253-aed0-dce7591e9442" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c7ac018-c572-51bb-a13b-07a9824c7d91" :question/math-academy-id "q-95722" :question/problem "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\nI. The probability that a student correctly solves $4$ questions in a $5$-question math test if the student has a $75%$ probability of correctly answering each question.\n\nII. The probability that $42$ flights out of $120$ arrive early at an airport if the probability of a single flight arriving early is $0.45$ for each flight.\n\nIII. The probability of getting $4$ ones on $10$ rolls of a $4$-sided die." :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have $n=5$ and $p=0.75.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =5\\cdot 0.75 \\\\  & =3.75 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation II, we have $n=120$ and $p=0.45.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =120\\cdot 0.45 \\\\  & =54 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =120\\cdot (1-0.45) \\\\  & =66 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation III, we have $n=10$ and $p=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =10\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =2.5 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115317 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91946709-3ca8-5501-be63-336b06f6c305" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Normal Approximation to Approximate a Binomial Probability Over an Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62b16514-c244-5b31-97f8-c74504e64a87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.149" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546465} {:answer/id #uuid "990b93e1-3cac-5f7d-b408-e57be8e31e56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546590} {:answer/id #uuid "cb05e951-7c6f-57f8-9e45-bf5c4d2a882d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.142" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546701} {:answer/id #uuid "ee565d64-1779-5a10-959b-2a050802e1ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.151" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546787} {:answer/id #uuid "f75cf845-2c9a-5c57-90ed-30300c515a53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.158" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546806}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb05e951-7c6f-57f8-9e45-bf5c4d2a882d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.142" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546701} :answer-field/id #uuid "b472d12d-8504-5350-ae9c-0cbfdecef465" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d478ba2f-45cc-54a9-9136-5867345b4ca9" :question/math-academy-id "q-95757" :question/problem "An archer practices firing an arrow at a target $60$ times. The archer has a $44%$ chance of hitting the bull's eye. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, estimate the probability that the archer hits the bull's eye more than $30$ times. Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8289\\,$ | $\\,0.8315\\,$ | $\\,0.8340\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8531\\,$ | $\\,0.8554\\,$ | $\\,0.8577\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8749\\,$ | $\\,0.8770\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=60$ and $p=0.44.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =60\\cdot 0.44 \\\\  & =26.4 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =60\\cdot (1-0.44) \\\\  & =33.6 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(60)(0.44)=26.4\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(60)(0.44)(1-0.44)=14.784.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(26.4,14.784).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(X>30).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X>30)=P(X\\ge 31)≈P(Y\\ge 30.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 30.5) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{30.5-26.4}{\\sqrt{14.784}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\ge 1.07) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<1.07) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.07) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.07) \\\\  & =1-0.8577 \\\\  & =0.1423\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X>30)≈0.142.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.1431.$"}]}
